{% set local_deep_dive %}
<p>Arab sits at the crossroads — literally between Huntsville and Guntersville, and geographically between the Tennessee Valley floor and the mountain plateaus to the east. At about 1,100 feet elevation on Brindlee Mountain, you're living in a different environment than folks down in the valley.</p>

<p>That elevation matters for drainage in ways people don't always consider:</p>

<p><strong>The Tennessee Valley Divide.</strong> Brindlee Mountain is a watershed divide. Rain that falls on the north side of the mountain drains toward the Tennessee River system. Rain on the south side drains toward the Mulberry Fork and eventually the Black Warrior River. Depending on exactly where your property sits, your drainage system is feeding one watershed or the other.</p>

<p><strong>The slope reality.</strong> Flat lots are rare in Arab. Most properties have some grade to them — sometimes subtle, sometimes dramatic. That slope affects everything about drainage. Water naturally accelerates downhill, concentrates in low spots, and finds paths of least resistance. Your gutters need to account for where that water wants to go.</p>

<p><strong>The soil difference.</strong> Mountain soils tend to be thinner than valley soils, with more rock beneath the surface. In some ways that helps drainage — rock channels water instead of holding it. In other ways it complicates underground drainage work. You can't always run pipes where you'd like to.</p>

<p><strong>The microclimate.</strong> At 1,100 feet, Arab is slightly cooler than Huntsville, slightly wetter in some years, and more prone to ice events in winter. That affects gutter wear patterns, freeze-thaw cycles, and how quickly organic debris breaks down in gutters.</p>

<p>Arab's position between Huntsville and Guntersville also makes it a natural commuter community. You're close enough to work in Huntsville (35-40 minutes), close enough to play at the lake (20 minutes to Guntersville), and still living in a small town where people know each other. That combination is drawing new residents — and new housing that brings its own drainage challenges.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set homeowner_guide %}
<p>Living in a small town like Arab means home care works a little differently than in a city suburb. Here's what's specific to taking care of an Arab home.</p>

<h4>The Slope Factor</h4>
<p>Most Arab properties aren't flat. If you're on the uphill side of your lot, your main concern is managing your own roof runoff. If you're on the downhill side, you're also dealing with runoff from uphill neighbors — and possibly from the road.</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>Uphill homes:</strong> Focus on getting water as far from the foundation as possible. Longer downspout extensions, underground drains if needed.</li>
<li><strong>Downhill homes:</strong> May need landscape-level drainage solutions beyond just gutters. French drains, swales, or retaining walls to redirect water that's coming from elsewhere.</li>
</ul>

<h4>Small Town Contractor Reality</h4>
<p>One challenge in smaller communities: fewer local contractors to choose from. The guys who are there often have more work than they can handle, which means slow scheduling and sometimes corners cut.</p>

