{% set local_spotlight %}
<p>Here's some trivia: Arab was supposed to be named "Arad" — after the son of the town's founder. But somewhere in the paperwork, it became "Arab" instead. The name stuck, and so has the town's character as a small, close-knit community.</p>

<h4>The Old Arab</h4>
<p>For most of its history, Arab was a quiet mountain town. A main street with local businesses. Churches that everyone went to. A school where multiple generations of families attended. Homes that stayed in families for decades.</p>

<p>Those older Arab homes — the ones built in the 50s, 60s, and 70s — tend to be practical. Ranch style, split-levels, modest but well-built. Many still have original gutters that were fine for 30-40 years but are well past retirement age now.</p>

<h4>The Growing Arab</h4>
<p>Arab has been discovered. Families who work in Huntsville have figured out that housing is more affordable here, schools are solid, and the commute isn't that bad. Retirees have found that the mountain air is nice and life is quieter than in the valley. Lake enthusiasts have realized Arab is an easy drive to Guntersville.</p>

<p>New subdivisions are appearing on what used to be farmland. The population has grown steadily. Arab is still small — about 8,500 people — but it's not as small as it used to be.</p>

<h4>What That Growth Means for Homes</h4>
<p>The new construction in Arab follows the same patterns we see everywhere: builder-grade materials, minimum-spec gutters, lots graded for quick construction rather than long-term drainage. Homes that look great on day one but start showing problems by year five.</p>

<p>Meanwhile, the older Arab homes need attention. Gutters that have served faithfully for 40 years are finally giving up. Fascia that looked fine last year is suddenly showing rot. Drainage patterns that worked before the subdivision went in behind your house have changed.</p>

<h4>Protecting Arab's Character</h4>
<p>Part of what makes Arab appealing is its small-town character. The older homes, the established trees, the sense of community. When we work on an Arab home, we're not just installing gutters — we're helping preserve what makes this town what it is.</p>

<p>Whether you're in a 1960s ranch that's been in your family for three generations or a brand-new home in one of the newer developments, we treat your property with respect. Good work, fair prices, and contractors who actually show up. That's what Arab deserves.</p>
